4} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The famous Hilton-Milner theorem settles the case where $\\\\mathcal{F}$ is $t$-intersecting.\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2506.21993, author = {Dehai Liu and Kaishun Wang and Tian Yao}, title = {$s$-almost cross-$t$-intersecting families for finite sets}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2506.21993}, note = {arXiv:2506.21993v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nTwo families $\\\\mathcal{F}$ and $\\\\mathcal{G}$ of $k$-subsets of an $n$-set are called $s$-almost cross-$t$-intersecting if each member in $\\\\mathcal{F}$ (resp. $\\\\mathcal{G}$) is $t$-disjoint with at most $s$ members in $\\\\mathcal{G}$ (resp. $\\\\mathcal{F}$). In this paper, we characterize the $s$-almost cross-$t$-intersecting families with the maximum product of their sizes. Furthermore, we provide a corresponding stability result after studying the $s$-almost cross-$t$-intersecting families which are not cross-$t$-intersecting.\\n\\n  * Flexible Bayesian Nonparametric Product Mixtures for Multi-scale Functional Clustering\\n\\nTsung-Hung Yao, Suprateek Kundu\\n\\nDec 16 2024 stat.ME math.ST stat.TH arXiv:2412.09792v1\\nFocusing on basis expansions for high-dimensional functions, we propose a flexible non-parametric Bayesian approach for multi-resolution clustering. The proposed method imposes independent Dirichlet process (DP) priors on different subsets of basis coefficients that ultimately results in a product of DP mixture priors inducing local clustering. We generalize the approach to incorporate spatially correlated error terms when modeling random spatial functions to provide improved model fitting. An efficient Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithm is developed for implementation. We show posterior consistency properties under the local clustering approach that asymptotically recovers the true density of random functions. Extensive simulations illustrate the improved clustering and function estimation under the proposed method compared to classical approaches. We apply the proposed approach to a spatial transcriptomics application where the goal is to infer clusters of genes with distinct spatial patterns of expressions. Our method makes an important contribution by expanding the limited literature on local clustering methods for high-dimensional functions with theoretical guarantees.\\n\\n  * $s$-almost $t$-intersecting families for finite sets\\n\\nDehai Liu, Kaishun Wang, Tian Yao\\n\\nOct 29 2024 math.CO arXiv:2410.20185v4\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2410.20185, author = {Dehai Liu and Kaishun Wang and Tian Yao}, title = {$s$-almost $t$-intersecting families for finite sets}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2410.20185}, note = {arXiv:2410.20185v4} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ns-almost cross-t-intersecting families for finite sets (https://www.znakschoola.ru/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2506.21993%23UE7DkG0PaBHZOakR54g1Wl8%3D)\\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Rado, Intersection theorems for systems of finite sets, Quart. ... Frankl, The Erd˝os-Ko-Rado theorem is true for n = ckt, in: I. ... cross-intersecting and almost cross-sperner pairs of families of sets, Graphs Combin. ... 2410.20185.\\nJ. Combin. 28 (2021)\\n#P2.7.\\n[11] P. Frankl and J. Wang, A product version of the Hilton-Milner-Frankl theorem, Sci.\\nChina Math. 67 (2024) 455–474.\\n[12] D. Gerbner, N. Lemons, C. Palmer, B. Patk´os and V. Sz´ecsi, Almost intersecting families\\nof sets, SIAM J. Discrete Math. 26 (2012) 1657–1699.\\n[13] D. Gerbner, N. Lemons, C. Palmer, D. P´alv¨olgyi, B. Patk´os and V. Sz´ecsi, Almost\\ncross-intersecting and almost cross-sperner pairs of families of sets, Graphs Combin. 29\\n(2013) 489–498.\\n[14] A. Hilton and E. Milner, Some intersection theorems for systems of finite sets, Quart.\\nJ. Math. Oxford Ser. (2) 18 (1967) 369–384.\\n[15] L. Ji, D. Liu, K. Wang, T. Yao an